{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Cleveland Guardians opened the year with a split-decision spring, stumbling through a rocky March and April that finished at a perfectly even 16-16. The early months were defined by a bullpen that couldn't hold leads — Cade Smith surrendered critical hits in back-to-back series, and Connor Brogdon gave away a gut-punch three-run homer on the same afternoon Daniel Schneemann had electrified the crowd with a grand slam. Still, young talent flashed through the noise: Chase DeLauter drove in runs in clutch moments, and Brayan Rocchio announced himself as a force with a three-run homer that salvaged an April series. The Guardians were a team still learning who they were.\n\nMay changed everything. Cleveland exploded to 18-11, their best month of the season, carried by a lineup that suddenly looked dangerous from top to bottom. Kyle Manzardo's solo homer in late May was a symbol of a team growing into its power, and the rotation gave the offense enough time to do damage. The Guardians rode that wave into June but crashed hard — a 10-15 month that exposed real bullpen vulnerabilities. Cade Smith was torched repeatedly, including a devastating late-game single on June 22nd that cost them dearly, and the Astros feasted on Matt Festa in a brutal mid-June loss. The June swoon left Cleveland scrambling to protect its division positioning.\n\nJuly and August were a grind — a combined 25-26, barely above water. But Brayan Rocchio became the heartbeat of the offense, launching a two-run walk-off homer on July 2nd that kept the ship from sinking entirely. The team leaned on pitching and small-ball execution, weathering the dog days without ever fully breaking down. September brought the surge Cleveland needed: a 16-9 final month powered by Rocchio's clutch two-run double, Petey Halpin's three-run blast, and a lineup that finally put it all together when the calendar demanded it most. The Guardians finished 85-77 and punched their ticket to October.\n\nThe postseason was a wild, emotionally charged run. Cleveland dispatched their Division Series opponent with José Ramírez and Jo Adell delivering in the biggest moments, setting up an ALCS showdown that pushed them to their limits. Nathaniel Lowe's go-ahead single in Game 1 of the Championship Series gave the city hope, but Tampa Bay proved relentless — Chandler Simpson's triple off Gavin Williams and Junior Caminero's double off Daniel Espino slowly turned the tide, and a Richie Palacios double in the final game sealed Cleveland's fate. The Guardians bowed out in the ALCS, their young roster bloodied but emboldened, leaving no doubt that this team's October story was far from its last chapter.",
